ah i think it's a  powerful message and i think again it  goes to that you know that that aspect  of um you know reaching across both  sides of the aisle um to to promote this  this technology  as someone who's in this space patrick  what are you going to be looking for and  listening into throughout the day as an  indication of where we might go and  where lawmakers might go with crypto  regulation moving forward  so to be honest um you know the the  questions and the if we can call them  battle lines that are being you know  drawn in that room among the you know  the the the legislators who are asking  their questions um  those are sort of as expected um i i  think i think that um you know no one's  no one has really gone off script here  um even even obviously among um you know  the folks who are um you know presenting  here so i i i think it again it it may  lead to congress feeling a little bit  more empowered to do things what those  things are remains to be seen um and um  you know and and maybe you know heading  into midterms um you know may we may see  a little bit of shifting of positions um  that i anticipate might be more  favorable to crypto  but that's hard to say as well sometimes  you you you get a hardening of a  position as you as you head into those  into those elections as well so um these  two leaves are a little bit difficult to  read right now interesting you think  crypto is going to play a big role in uh  election outcomes as we head into the  midterms  well you know i think um maybe not  directly yeah but indirectly if you if  you look at what what is really being  discussed here these are huge and  momentous issues we're the very nature  of banking and payments is being  discussed here um the different manners  by the different ways by which different  regulate regulatory agencies regulate  that's on that that's being discussed  here um the geopolitical clash between  china and the us  is being discussed here the future of  the world of th